WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.475
Block three.

2
00:00:01.475 --> 00:00:04.650
Overfitting, and is the improvement real?

3
00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:13.000
On the page, this block is the sections "Why not make the network enormous?", "What can we do about overfitting?" and "Is the improvement real?".

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.225
Block two was about training failing to work.

5
00:00:16.225 --> 00:00:23.625
This block is about training working too well: the model fits the training data beautifully and then fails on anything new.

6
00:00:23.625 --> 00:00:33.650
We will take Session eight's localisation network, watch it memorise, try five different remedies, and find that the honest answer is not the one people hope for.

7
00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:43.150
Then the tools themselves, L1, L2 and dropout, and finally where all of this sits in a world where almost nobody trains a model from scratch any more.

8
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.300
Back to the network we built at the end of Session eight.

9
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:54.350
The input is a protein sequence: a hundred and fifty residues, one-hot encoded, three thousand numbers.

10
00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.200
A hidden layer of sixty-four units.

11
00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.575
Five output classes.

12
00:00:59.575 --> 00:01:03.625
And the training set is four hundred and eighty-six proteins.

13
00:01:03.625 --> 00:01:08.150
Put those two facts next to each other, which is what the slide is for.

14
00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:12.450
The first layer alone has three thousand times sixty-four weights.

15
00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:17.775
The whole network has a hundred and ninety-two thousand, three hundred and eighty-nine.

16
00:01:17.775 --> 00:01:24.250
A hundred and ninety-two thousand free parameters, fitted on four hundred and eighty-six examples.

17
00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.325
Roughly four hundred weights per example.

18
00:01:27.325 --> 00:01:30.825
You do not need any theory to be nervous about that.

19
00:01:30.825 --> 00:01:40.350
There is enough freedom in that model to describe each training protein individually, and when a model can do that, there is no pressure on it to find anything general.

20
00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:46.025
Before I show you what happens, predict what the training and validation curves will look like.

21
00:01:46.625 --> 00:01:50.950
Here it is, and it is overfitting in its plainest possible form.

22
00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:54.725
Training accuracy: one point zero zero zero.

23
00:01:54.725 --> 00:01:58.550
Every single training protein classified correctly.

24
00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:02.725
The model has memorised its four hundred and eighty-six examples.

25
00:02:02.725 --> 00:02:09.025
Validation accuracy peaks at epoch five, and then falls, ending at nought point four three eight.

26
00:02:09.025 --> 00:02:10.600
Epoch five.

27
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:22.950
The network learns whatever generalisable signal it is going to learn in the first handful of passes over the data, and everything after that is memorisation, which actively makes it worse on new proteins.

28
00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:26.475
That is worth understanding rather than just naming.

29
00:02:26.475 --> 00:02:40.750
The network is not doing anything wrong in its own terms: it is reducing the training loss, which is what we told it to do, and the most efficient remaining way to reduce the training loss is to learn the idiosyncrasies of these particular sequences.

30
00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:46.500
A quirk of one training protein is, to the model, just another pattern that predicts a label.

31
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.675
And notice the two curves tell you this clearly, long before you look at a test set.

32
00:02:51.675 --> 00:02:57.725
Divergence between training and validation loss is the signature, and it is why you plot both.

33
00:02:58.325 --> 00:03:01.425
And now the result that should keep you humble.

34
00:03:01.425 --> 00:03:05.975
On this data, plain logistic regression beats the neural network.

35
00:03:05.975 --> 00:03:12.275
Let me put the numbers in context first, because they are all low and I do not want you to mis-read them.

36
00:03:12.275 --> 00:03:17.250
Chance accuracy, with five balanced classes, is nought point two zero.

37
00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:25.025
The untrained network from Session eight scored nought point one eight one with an MCC of minus nought point zero two seven.

38
00:03:25.025 --> 00:03:32.400
So every trained model here is far above chance; the problem is genuinely hard, and we are comparing real models.

39
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.400
And the simplest of them is the best one.

40
00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:47.575
This is why Session eight insisted on baselines, and I want to state the rule in its strongest form: before you believe that your bigger model is better, compare it with the simplest model that could possibly work.

41
00:03:47.575 --> 00:03:52.600
Logistic regression, or predicting the most common class, or nearest-neighbour.

42
00:03:52.600 --> 00:04:00.275
If your network does not beat those convincingly, you do not have a result, whatever the network's absolute numbers look like.

43
00:04:00.275 --> 00:04:05.400
It is an uncomfortable habit because it so often deflates a week of work.

44
00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.475
It is also the difference between a finding and an artefact.

45
00:04:10.075 --> 00:04:12.150
So what can be done?

46
00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:19.475
Five remedies, and I have put them in order of how much they actually help, which is not the order people usually try them in.

47
00:04:19.475 --> 00:04:21.500
One: more data.

48
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:26.100
By a wide margin the most reliable fix, and the least glamorous.

49
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:32.425
More examples make memorisation harder and generalisation easier, with no tuning required.

50
00:04:32.425 --> 00:04:35.875
Two: a smaller model, or a better architecture.

51
00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:38.775
Fewer parameters to memorise with.

52
00:04:38.775 --> 00:04:54.725
Or, better, an architecture whose built-in assumptions do the work instead of free parameters, which is Session ten: its convolutional network does this same task with nineteen thousand, two hundred and thirty-seven weights instead of a hundred and ninety-two thousand.

53
00:04:54.725 --> 00:04:56.925
Three: early stopping.

54
00:04:56.925 --> 00:05:01.750
Keep the weights from the best validation epoch rather than the last one.

55
00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:05.175
Almost free, and often most of the benefit.

56
00:05:05.175 --> 00:05:09.525
Four: weight decay, also called L2, or its relative L1.

57
00:05:09.525 --> 00:05:11.825
Penalise large weights.

58
00:05:11.825 --> 00:05:13.750
Five: dropout.

59
00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:16.875
Switch off random units during training.

60
00:05:16.875 --> 00:05:21.950
And now the honest result on our localisation network, which is on the slide.

61
00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:25.525
Early stopping alone gives nought point four eight six.

62
00:05:25.525 --> 00:05:30.325
Adding dropout and weight decay on top gives nought point four seven six.

63
00:05:30.325 --> 00:05:32.175
Slightly worse.

64
00:05:32.175 --> 00:05:35.350
The fancy regularisation bought nothing.

65
00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:43.150
And here is the part I most want you to take away: whatever we do, training accuracy still reaches one point zero zero zero.

66
00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:53.175
A hundred and ninety-two thousand weights can always memorise four hundred and eighty-six examples; regularisation changes how it memorises, not whether it can.

67
00:05:53.175 --> 00:05:59.975
Regularisation is not a substitute for data, and it is not a substitute for a model that fits the problem.

68
00:05:59.975 --> 00:06:03.050
It is a modest improvement at the margin.

69
00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:08.950
If your model has four hundred weights per training example, no amount of dropout will save you.

70
00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:14.400
Before the individual tools, the habit that ties the whole session together.

71
00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:19.625
Plot two curves, always: training loss and validation loss, against epoch.

72
00:06:19.625 --> 00:06:23.175
They answer two different questions, and you need both.

73
00:06:23.175 --> 00:06:27.525
The training loss answers: is optimisation working at all?

74
00:06:27.525 --> 00:06:31.750
If the training loss is not falling, nothing further matters.

75
00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:40.800
Your model cannot even fit the data it can see, and the cause is somewhere in Block two: initialisation, vanishing gradients, learning rate, or a bug.

76
00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:46.150
The validation loss answers: does more optimisation still help on new data?

77
00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:49.350
While it falls, training is buying you something.

78
00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:57.950
When it flattens and then rises, while the training loss continues down, the network has stopped learning the problem and started learning the training set.

79
00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:07.850
So the recipe is: keep the weights from the epoch with the best validation loss, which was fifty-two for the breast-cancer network and five for the localisation network.

80
00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:10.600
And then the discipline from Session eight.

81
00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:17.600
Because you chose that epoch using the validation set, the validation score at that epoch is optimistic.

82
00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:21.400
Only the test set, looked at once, gives an honest number.

83
00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:29.325
These two curves are the first thing I look at in anyone's project, and they usually tell me what is wrong before I have read any of the code.

84
00:07:29.925 --> 00:07:37.700
Now the two penalties, which are worth understanding properly because the difference between them is useful rather than cosmetic.

85
00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:44.950
Both work by adding a term to the loss, so that the optimiser is pushed to keep the weights small as well as to fit the data.

86
00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:50.750
L2, which PyTorch calls weight decay, adds lambda times the sum of the squared weights.

87
00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:54.825
L1 adds lambda times the sum of the absolute values.

88
00:07:54.825 --> 00:07:58.325
Lambda is a knob you choose on the validation set.

89
00:07:58.325 --> 00:08:01.425
Now, the question that reveals the difference.

90
00:08:01.425 --> 00:08:05.750
Which penalty still pulls hard on a weight that is already very small?

91
00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:07.950
Work out the derivative.

92
00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:12.425
For L2, the pull on a weight is two lambda times the weight itself.

93
00:08:12.425 --> 00:08:19.000
So as the weight shrinks, the pull shrinks with it, and it approaches zero asymptotically but never arrives.

94
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:26.100
That is also why it is called weight decay: every step multiplies the weights by a factor slightly less than one.

95
00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:31.450
For L1, the pull is lambda, a constant, regardless of how small the weight is.

96
00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:37.400
So the pull does not fade, and it pushes weights all the way to exactly zero, where they stop.

97
00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:41.500
That is the whole difference, and it has a practical consequence.

98
00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:44.850
L2 shrinks everything and keeps everything.

99
00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:52.550
L1 produces a sparse model: many weights become exactly zero, and the features attached to them have been discarded.

100
00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:59.400
If you have met these in statistics under other names: L2 is ridge regression, L1 is the lasso.

101
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.525
And here is why you might choose the sparse one, on the biopsy data.

102
00:09:04.525 --> 00:09:12.800
With L2, the best lambda is three point nine eight, all thirty weights survive, and the test accuracy is nought point nine six five.

103
00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:21.125
With L1, the best lambda is fifteen point eight, six of the thirty weights survive, and the test accuracy is nought point nine four two.

104
00:09:21.125 --> 00:09:28.150
So L1 costs you about two percentage points and gives you a model that uses six measurements instead of thirty.

105
00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:32.200
Whether that is a good trade is not a statistical question.

106
00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:38.200
A model with six features can be printed on a card, checked by a pathologist, and argued about.

107
00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:45.175
Those six features are a hypothesis about what distinguishes malignant nuclei, and someone can go and test it.

108
00:09:45.175 --> 00:09:50.775
A model with thirty weighted features is a black box that happens to work slightly better.

109
00:09:50.775 --> 00:09:55.200
And in some settings L1 is not a preference but a necessity.

110
00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:01.700
Gene-expression data is the standard case: twenty thousand genes measured on a few hundred patients.

111
00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:08.800
Without something forcing sparsity, you have far more features than examples and the model will fit noise beautifully.

112
00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:15.600
L1 picks a handful of genes, which is both statistically necessary and biologically interpretable.

113
00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:21.125
The third discussion problem asks you to check the effect of the two penalties by hand.

114
00:10:21.725 --> 00:10:25.450
Dropout, which is the strangest-sounding of the remedies.

115
00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:34.250
During training, each hidden unit is switched off at random with some probability, commonly a half, with a fresh random choice for every mini-batch.

116
00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:37.125
At test time, every unit is on.

117
00:10:37.125 --> 00:10:44.550
Before I explain, ask yourself the obvious question: why on earth would damaging the network during training make it better?

118
00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:46.750
Here is the argument.

119
00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:50.950
Without dropout, hidden units can form fragile coalitions.

120
00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:59.900
Unit seventeen learns to do half a job, relying on unit forty-two to do the other half, and the combination fits some quirk of the training data.

121
00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:06.125
That coalition is brittle: it is specific to the training set, and it will not hold on new data.

122
00:11:06.125 --> 00:11:13.750
With dropout, unit seventeen cannot rely on unit forty-two, because on the next batch unit forty-two may be missing.

123
00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:24.450
So each unit has to compute something that is useful on its own, and the information gets spread redundantly across many units instead of concentrated in fragile combinations.

124
00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:27.875
On GB1 you can watch the trade directly.

125
00:11:27.875 --> 00:11:41.050
With dropout at nought point two, the training mean absolute error gets worse, from nought point two one two to nought point two two zero, and the test error gets better, from nought point two five four to nought point two four seven.

126
00:11:41.050 --> 00:11:51.450
That is the signature of a regularisation method working as intended: you deliberately make the model fit the training data less well, in exchange for fitting new data better.

127
00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:56.400
If a technique improves your training score, it is not regularising anything.

128
00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:02.175
One practical trap, which costs people an afternoon and which you can now avoid.

129
00:12:02.175 --> 00:12:06.250
Dropout must be on during training and off at evaluation.

130
00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:14.825
In PyTorch you control that with model dot train and model dot eval, and these also affect other layers such as batch normalisation.

131
00:12:14.825 --> 00:12:23.100
If you forget model dot eval before evaluating, dropout stays on, which means units are being randomly switched off while you measure.

132
00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:31.275
Your accuracy will be worse than it should be, and, more confusingly, it will be different every time you run it, because the random choice differs.

133
00:12:31.275 --> 00:12:37.500
If you see an evaluation that changes between identical runs, this is the first thing to check.

134
00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:41.725
One detail of how it works, so the behaviour is not mysterious.

135
00:12:41.725 --> 00:12:47.175
During training, PyTorch scales the surviving units up by one over one minus p.

136
00:12:47.175 --> 00:12:55.900
That keeps the total signal at roughly the same size whether half the units are off or none are, which is why nothing has to be adjusted at test time.

137
00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:59.925
Every training loop in this course calls both train and eval.

138
00:12:59.925 --> 00:13:02.075
Make it a habit now.

139
00:13:02.675 --> 00:13:09.225
Let us put the three datasets side by side, because together they say something that none of them says alone.

140
00:13:09.225 --> 00:13:10.950
Breast cancer.

141
00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:15.900
The data is nearly additive, and there is plenty of it relative to the model.

142
00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:19.375
A hidden layer ties with logistic regression.

143
00:13:19.375 --> 00:13:22.250
The flexibility had nothing to do.

144
00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:23.975
GB1.

145
00:13:23.975 --> 00:13:30.600
The data is genuinely non-additive, because of epistasis, and there are twenty thousand training examples.

146
00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:36.250
The network wins decisively, nought point seven nine against nought point five zero.

147
00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:38.200
Localisation.

148
00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:46.500
The signal is presumably non-additive too, but there are four hundred and eighty-six proteins and a hundred and ninety-two thousand weights.

149
00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:50.700
The network memorises, and loses to logistic regression.

150
00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:59.900
So the answer to should I use a neural network is: it depends on whether the structure of your problem needs one, and on whether you have the data to pay for it.

151
00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:01.800
Both, not either.

152
00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:08.250
One caveat about the examples-per-weight heuristic, because I do not want you to over-apply it.

153
00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:10.950
It is a warning sign, not a rule.

154
00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:21.625
Modern networks routinely have more parameters than training examples and generalise perfectly well, because of their architecture, their regularisation and the way they are trained.

155
00:14:21.625 --> 00:14:25.950
The ratio is a reason to look carefully, not a proof of failure.

156
00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:34.975
And Session eight's rules apply to every one of these comparisons: choose on validation, test once, and always compare against a simple baseline.

157
00:14:35.575 --> 00:14:39.375
A last demonstration of that discipline, from your lab.

158
00:14:39.375 --> 00:14:41.650
Four settings were tried.

159
00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:46.050
The best one scored nought point eight two three on the validation set.

160
00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:51.700
That setting, evaluated once on the test set, scored nought point seven seven six.

161
00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:54.350
Five percentage points lower.

162
00:14:54.350 --> 00:15:02.400
And that is not a failure or an error: it is the expected consequence of the fact that the choice itself was fitted to the validation set.

163
00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:07.925
You selected the best of four noisy numbers, so the one you selected is optimistic.

164
00:15:07.925 --> 00:15:18.100
If you report nought point eight two three as what your method achieves, you are overstating it, and you will have a confusing conversation when someone else cannot reproduce it.

165
00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:21.750
The honest number is nought point seven seven six.

166
00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:31.725
The more options you try, the bigger that gap becomes, which is a reason to be a bit sceptical of very large hyperparameter searches reported with validation numbers.

167
00:15:31.725 --> 00:15:38.700
Session ten's lab trains a convolutional network on these same blood cells, so the comparison carries forward.

168
00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:47.950
Before the last slide, a nice piece of bookkeeping: everything in the second half of this course, assembled in one method from nineteen ninety-three.

169
00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:52.725
PHD, which you met in Session seven, predicts secondary structure.

170
00:15:52.725 --> 00:15:55.200
Look at what it is made of.

171
00:15:55.200 --> 00:16:00.375
Its input is a profile from a multiple sequence alignment: Session five.

172
00:16:00.375 --> 00:16:05.050
Its architecture is a network with hidden layers: Session seven.

173
00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:10.350
Its output is three classes with the appropriate loss and metrics: Session eight.

174
00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:13.850
It is trained by backpropagation: this session.

175
00:16:13.850 --> 00:16:25.250
And it was tested on sets purged of sequences similar to the training set: Session eight's homology partitioning, in nineteen ninety-three, which was ahead of its time and is still ahead of some papers.

176
00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:28.825
Seventy point eight per cent three-state accuracy.

177
00:16:28.825 --> 00:16:34.525
I show this because it is easy to experience a course as a list of disconnected techniques.

178
00:16:34.525 --> 00:16:42.225
PHD is one method that needs every single one of them, and its authors got the methodology right as well as the method.

179
00:16:42.825 --> 00:16:50.450
And a last slide about the world you are actually going into, because it would be misleading to end on a network with five hundred weights.

180
00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:56.775
The training loop from Block one of this session is the same loop used for the largest models that exist.

181
00:16:56.775 --> 00:16:58.575
Five lines.

182
00:16:58.575 --> 00:17:02.725
What changed is scale, and the scale is difficult to convey.

183
00:17:02.725 --> 00:17:15.375
Kaplan and colleagues showed in twenty twenty that a language model's loss falls as a power law with model size, data and compute: make everything bigger in the right proportion and the loss goes down predictably.

184
00:17:15.375 --> 00:17:18.375
Protein models followed the same path.

185
00:17:18.375 --> 00:17:27.175
ESM-2 went from eight million to fifteen billion parameters, and structural information emerged in its representations as it grew.

186
00:17:27.175 --> 00:17:35.775
Evo 2 has forty billion parameters, trained on about nine trillion bases of DNA, on two thousand and forty-eight GPUs.

187
00:17:35.775 --> 00:17:48.050
ESM3 has ninety-eight billion parameters and was trained with one point zero seven times ten to the twenty-four floating-point operations, on two point seven eight billion proteins.

188
00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:50.800
Ten to the twenty-four operations.

189
00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:53.825
Essentially nobody can repeat that.

190
00:17:53.825 --> 00:18:03.275
So what almost everybody actually does, including most academic groups, is start from a pretrained model and fine-tune it on their own small dataset.

191
00:18:03.275 --> 00:18:09.350
Often with a method called LoRA, which trains small low-rank updates instead of all the weights.

192
00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:13.050
And here is why this session still matters in that world.

193
00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:24.575
Fine-tuning on a small biological dataset is exactly the situation where today's tools are needed: a validation set, early stopping, weight decay, dropout, and the discipline of a baseline.

194
00:18:24.575 --> 00:18:31.475
The overfitting problem is worse, not better, when you adapt a billion-parameter model to four hundred examples.

195
00:18:31.475 --> 00:18:36.800
Then the question on the slide, which is a real one and which I would like you to think about.

196
00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:41.725
Only a handful of companies and large laboratories can train these models.

197
00:18:41.725 --> 00:18:48.475
Some release their weights openly, such as ESM-2 and Evo 2, which lets everyone else build on them.

198
00:18:48.475 --> 00:18:50.300
Others do not.

199
00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:55.825
So: who can afford the next model, and what does that mean for open science in our field?

200
00:18:55.825 --> 00:18:58.650
I do not have a comfortable answer.

201
00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:01.375
That is Session nine.

202
00:19:01.375 --> 00:19:09.050
You can now train a network, recognise the three common ways training fails, and tell whether an apparent improvement is real.

203
00:19:09.050 --> 00:19:13.275
Session ten changes the architecture rather than the training.

204
00:19:13.275 --> 00:19:29.175
Convolutional networks assume that nearby inputs belong together and reuse one small detector everywhere, and that single assumption lets them do this session's localisation task with nineteen thousand weights instead of a hundred and ninety-two thousand, and beat it.

205
00:19:29.175 --> 00:19:36.425
It is also the session where you get to open a network up and find a piece of biology inside it that nobody put there.

206
00:19:36.425 --> 00:19:45.200
The Session nine page has all of this written out, and its "Check your understanding" section at the end is the fastest way to find out whether it stuck.

207
00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:49.250
The course notebook is linked in the box under the page's title.

208
00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:50.875
See you there.

